# Records that replace what tools/sdkgen's rules would write, in the database's
# own syntax: special, var, data, stub and fn records for one export each, and
# the shape and struct records that let a structure of callbacks cross.  Two
# kinds exist only here.  omit <export> leaves the export out of the database,
# so a guest importing it fails to bind with 71 and the name.  opaque <record>
# names a structure the guest never builds itself: the rule that refuses a
# pointer to a structure holding function pointers ignores it, because the
# function pointers in it were put there by native code, while the layout rule
# still holds it to the same size, alignment and field offsets on both
# architectures.  A record applies to the library named by the library line
# above it.  A stub may name its exports with a fnmatch pattern, which only
# reaches exports the rules would have made fn or stub, never a variable; a
# stub that names one export exactly also covers that export's $-suffixed
# variants, such as _sigsuspend$NOCANCEL.  An exact name beats a pattern, so a
# special for one member of a family survives a stub that names the rest.  A
# record that matches nothing in its library stops the generator, so a name
# that leaves the SDK is noticed rather than kept.
#
# inplace <export> <argpos> <offset> <signature> is for a structure the guest
# owns and the native function works in, a compression stream above all: zlib
# reads its input and writes its progress in the z_stream the caller holds, so
# the structure cannot be copied as a shape is, and two of its words are the
# caller's allocation functions.  The record names one such word, which the
# bridge converts where it lies for the length of the call and puts back
# afterwards unless the native function stored something of its own there, as
# zlib does when it finds the word empty.  It also lifts the rule that would
# have stubbed the export for taking such a structure.
#
# ---- format veneers ----
# A variadic function cannot cross by signature: Apple's arm64 passes every
# variadic argument on the stack while x86-64 passes the first ones in
# registers, and nothing in the declaration says how many there are or of which
# class.  A formatting function says it anyway, in its format string.  So the
# few that programs call all the time are special records whose handlers ocerz
# implements as format veneers: the handler reads the format string out of
# guest memory, walks its conversions to learn the class of each argument that
# follows, gathers them from the x86 registers and stack in that order, and
# makes the native call with them where arm64 wants them.  The group is printf,
# fprintf, sprintf, snprintf, asprintf, dprintf, syslog, warn, warnx, the wide
# swprintf, wprintf and fwprintf, whose format is read a wchar_t at a time, and
# the fortified __sprintf_chk and __snprintf_chk in libSystem,
# CFStringCreateWithFormat and
# CFStringAppendFormat in CoreFoundation, and NSLog in Foundation, whose %@
# conversions are object pointers like any other.  Every other variadic
# function, and every v form taking a va_list, stays a stub, apart from the few
# whose ellipsis stands for one fixed argument, which the libSystem section
# below makes special records of their own.
#
# ---- libSystem ----
# Exports ocerz answers itself.  exit and abort end the guest, not the host
# thread; __stack_chk_fail reports the overrun; __tlv_bootstrap returns the
# guest's own thread-local storage and must keep every register but rax; the
# signal calls work on the guest's handler table, masks and alternate stacks,
# which the host's never see.  ___stack_chk_guard is a slot of the image's own
# with a random canary.  ___chkstk_darwin is the stack probe clang calls from a
# function with a frame larger than a page, with the size in rax and every
# register to come back unchanged; guest stacks are mapped whole, so its
# handler only returns.  Current clang imports it as ____chkstk_darwin, one
# underscore longer, which libSystem exports beside the older spelling.  exit
# takes the exit handler, which calls the host's exit while the guest is still
# running, so the guest's atexit handlers run as the callbacks they are.  _exit
# and _Exit end the process without atexit handlers, and native ones would end
# it without ocerz's own exit path either, so they take exit_now, which ends
# the process with the status and flushes nothing; quick_exit would have to run
# at_quick_exit handlers first, which neither handler does, so it names itself
# instead.
#
# Stdio.  A FILE is a struct __sFILE that only native fopen, fdopen, funopen and
# their relatives create, and the _close, _read, _seek and _write pointers in
# it are native code or, for funopen, callbacks that crossed as ordinary c
# arguments.  So __sFILE is opaque.  Its layout still has to agree, and does,
# because the x86 getc and putc macros read _p, _r and _w out of the native
# FILE inline.
#
# Writable variables that native code calls through.  vprintf_stderr_func is the
# function Mach's fprintf_stderr writes its error messages with, and mach_init.h
# invites applications to point it at their own; a guest that does would have
# native code jump to x86, so it is left out.  _DefaultRuneLocale is the only
# other variable the SDK declares writably with a function pointer in its type,
# its __sgetrune and __sputrune words, and it stays a native data export: x86
# ctype macros read __runetype out of it inline, so leaving it out would refuse
# every program that calls isspace, and nothing but native setlocale ever writes
# it.
#
# Fortified string calls.  A compiler that knows a buffer's size calls
# __memcpy_chk and its siblings instead of memcpy, but the SDK only declares
# the builtins that expand to them, so no header gives a signature; these are
# the builtins' own, the plain function's arguments plus the destination size.
# __bzero is libsystem_platform's entry for bzero, at the same address.  It is
# declared void, but on x86_64 it shares its body with memset and leaves the
# destination in rax, and compiled code relies on that: clang turns a function
# that clears a buffer and returns it into a tail call to __bzero and lets the
# callee's rax be the result.  A crossing under v(pL) writes no result, so the
# caller read whatever rax last held.  bzero and __bzero are therefore special,
# and their handler answers the destination.
#
# Process creation and replacement: fork, vfork and forkpty would duplicate
# ocerz's translator state with no guest-side bookkeeping, and the exec family,
# posix_spawn and everything that configures it, system, popen and pclose,
# daemon and wordexp, which runs a shell, would start an x86 image with no
# translator under it.  ocerz has to launch those through itself.
#
# Thread cancellation: pthread_cancel, pthread_testcancel and dispatch_main,
# which parks the main thread for good, unwind a host thread through ocerz's own
# frames.  __tlv_atexit and __tlv_exit belong to the thread-local storage ocerz
# keeps for the guest, not to host dyld's.
#
# Private entry points.  The compiler's out-of-line atomics, the guarded file
# descriptor calls Chromium wraps every descriptor in, the responsibility and
# sandbox calls and proc_get_wakemon_params are in no SDK header, so the rules
# can only stub them, but their signatures are fixed and public in Apple's open
# sources, and they cross under those.  sandbox_check is variadic with at most
# one pointer after its filter type, and abort_report_np takes a format; each
# has a handler.  sandbox_init, sandbox_init_with_parameters, libsandbox's
# sandbox_apply and __sandbox_ms, which Chromium calls itself to apply a profile
# its browser process compiled, confine ocerz along with the guest, and a
# profile that denies reading outside the program's own files denies ocerz its
# databases, so their handlers load every database before the native call is
# made.  The exception-port calls are recorded and answered with success
# and no previous handlers, never installed: a fault in translated code has to
# reach ocerz's own handler, which a port set on the task would take first.
# mach_vm_map and mach_vm_remap, and their vm_ spellings, reach the host kernel:
# native mode runs in the identity map, where an address the kernel chooses is
# one the guest can use, and a fixed address first loses whatever translations
# were made from it.  IOKit still exports the SCDynamicStore and SCPreferences
# calls it held before SystemConfiguration took them, and a program linked
# against an old SDK binds them there: IOKit's header pass reads
# SystemConfiguration's headers so they cross under the same signatures, and the
# store's context structure is recorded for IOKit as well.
#
# Thread-specific data.  On x86_64 macOS pthread_getspecific is one instruction,
# a load from gs at eight times the key, and compilers and libraries inline it:
# Chromium reads its thread-local slots that way eighty times over.  A key made
# by the host's pthread_key_create names a slot in the host thread's storage,
# which no gs-relative load in guest code can reach, so a value stored through
# the function read back as null through the inline load.  pthread_key_create,
# pthread_key_delete, pthread_setspecific and pthread_getspecific are special:
# the keys are ocerz's own, numbered 256 to 767 as the dynamic keys of the real
# library are, and their values live at those slots of the guest thread block
# gs points into, where both the function and the instruction find them.
# pthread_create is special so that the start routine runs inside a frame of
# ocerz's that calls the keys' destructors, as guest code, when the routine
# returns, and pthread_exit is special so that it runs them too before it ends
# the host thread.  errno is thread-specific data of the same kind: ___error
# answers a slot of the guest thread block, which ocerz copies to and from the
# host's errno at the edges of every native call, because between two guest
# instructions ocerz's own code runs on the same host thread and leaves its own
# errors behind.
#
# The page size.  This host's pages are 16 KB and an Intel Mac's are 4 KB, which
# x86 programs are compiled knowing: Chromium's allocator works in 4 KB pages by
# constant while V8 beside it asks sysconf, and stops when the two disagree.
# ocerz's memory layer gives the guest 4 KB pages over the host's, so everything
# that tells the guest the page size tells it 4096: getpagesize, sysconf for
# _SC_PAGESIZE, host_page_size, the hw.pagesize and vm.pagesize sysctls, and
# vm_page_size, vm_kernel_page_size and their masks and shifts, which are
# variables of the image's own rather than the host's.
#
# Stopping a thread.  thread_suspend and thread_resume go through ocerz's own
# suspension, as thread_get_state already does: a guest thread stopped by the
# kernel wherever it happens to be may be holding the translator's lock, and
# then the thread that stopped it waits for that lock the first time it needs a
# block translated, which is a program's sampling profiler freezing the whole
# process.  ocerz stops a thread only outside the translator, and takes one that
# is inside a native call as it is, with the guest registers it crossed with.
#
# The stack.  Guest code runs on a stack ocerz made for it, not on its host
# thread's, so pthread_get_stackaddr_np and pthread_get_stacksize_np answer that
# stack for a thread that has one: a collector that scans from its stack pointer
# up to the address the first gives, as V8's does, would otherwise be handed a
# top below where it stands.
#
# Malloc zones.  A zone is a structure of function pointers, and a program may
# do more than call through one: Chromium saves the default zone's pointers and
# writes its own over them, so that every allocation passes its out-of-memory
# check.  Written into the host's zone those x86 pointers are the next thing the
# host's own calloc jumps to.  The guest therefore never holds a host zone: each
# call that answers one - malloc_default_zone, malloc_default_purgeable_zone,
# malloc_create_zone, malloc_get_all_zones, malloc_zone_from_ptr - answers a
# view of it, a zone structure in a page of the guest's own, the same view every
# time, whose pointers are thunks the guest can call and which reach the host
# zone's functions with the host zone in place of the view.  What the guest
# writes into a view changes what the guest's own malloc_zone_ calls do and
# nothing else.  A zone the guest registers is recorded and never registered
# with the host's allocator, which would call it.  The malloc_zone_ calls look
# at the zone they are given: where its pointer is the guest's, in a zone of its
# own or written over a view's, the call becomes a jump to that function with
# the arguments where they already are, and otherwise it is the host call on the
# host zone.  malloc_zone_from_ptr asks the host, and then each guest zone's own
# size function.  malloc_set_zone_name writes the name into a guest zone or a
# view itself, because the host's would leave the zone's page read-only, and a
# host page is four guest pages.
#
# Non-local jumps and contexts: setjmp and longjmp in every spelling, and the
# ucontext calls, save or restore arm64 host registers where the guest meant
# its own x86 ones.  Unwinding is the same problem for C++: _Unwind_*, the unw_*
# cursor API and frame registration walk host frames, not guest frames.
#
# Dynamic loading.  Host dyld has never heard of an image ocerz's loader
# mapped, and the host's dlopen would load arm64 code, so none of this family
# can cross.  dlopen, dlopen_audited, dlopen_from, dlopen_preflight, dlsym,
# dladdr, dlclose and dlerror are special records ocerz answers from its own
# loader: a guest x86_64 dylib or bundle is loaded, bound and initialized the
# way one linked at launch is, a library a database describes answers with its
# synthesized image, and an arm64 library with no database is refused by name.
# So is the half of the dyld API a program walks its images with: the count,
# the header, name and slide of an index, the slide of a header, the add-image
# and remove-image registrations, the image containing an address as a header,
# a path or a yes or no, and the program's own header.  The program's SDK,
# minimum OS and platform and their at-least forms are answered from the guest
# main image's LC_BUILD_VERSION, and the per-image forms for any image the
# loader holds.  _dyld_is_memory_immutable answers from a guest image's segment
# protection and asks host dyld about native memory.  Native mode has no x86
# shared cache, so dyld_shared_cache_some_image_overridden answers no, and
# _dyld_shared_cache_contains_path answers yes exactly for the libraries a
# database describes, which stand where the cache's did and are no more on disk
# than those were: a caller that asks before it dlopens, as ctypes does, finds
# them.  Every other _dyld_* and dyld_* call stays a stub - the UUID and
# section queries, process info, the shared-cache iterators, the Objective-C
# and Swift preoptimization hooks, pseudo-dylibs, the fork hooks and the old
# lookup-and-bind calls describe host dyld's own images and tables, none of
# which is the guest's - and so does the NSModule-era API.  The getsect and
# get_end family read the host's main executable, which is ocerz.
# dyld_stub_binder is only reached by a lazy bind the loader failed to make, so
# a stub that names it is the honest answer.
#
# Process identity: host dyld's _NSGetExecutablePath names ocerz's executable and
# _NSGetMachExecuteHeader ocerz's header, so both are specials answering the
# guest's.  The rest of crt_externs, _NSGetArgc, _NSGetArgv, _NSGetEnviron and
# _NSGetProgname, and getprogname and setprogname cross by rule: ocerz points the
# host's argc, argv and program name at the guest's before any framework opens,
# and they answer the same variables the _NXArgc, _NXArgv, _environ and
# ___progname data exports name.
#
# Memory mapping and protection: mmap, munmap, mprotect, madvise and the Mach
# vm allocate, deallocate, protect, map and remap calls change the address
# space ocerz tracks for the guest, including the pages it translated code
# from, without ocerz hearing of it.
#
# Raw syscalls: syscall and __syscall would reach the host kernel with x86
# system call numbers.
#
# The rest of the signal interface: sigsuspend, sigwait, sigpending, pause,
# sigvec, sigblock, sigsetmask, the System V sigset, sighold, sigrelse,
# sigignore, sigpause and siginterrupt, bsd_signal, and the private
# double-underscore entries behind them all read or wait on the host's signal
# state, which is not the guest's.
#
# Machine state and exception ports: thread_get_state, thread_set_state and
# their act_ and task_ spellings take x86 thread-state flavors the arm64 kernel
# refuses or answers with arm64 registers, and thread_create_running starts a
# host thread from x86 state.  Exception ports set by the guest would take
# EXC_BAD_ACCESS away from the signal handlers ocerz recovers translated code
# with.
#
# The printf and scanf families are variadic, and their v forms take a
# va_list, so the rules already refuse nearly all of them; naming the family
# keeps the extensible-printf domain calls and the _l, wide and _chk forms with
# them.  The eight format veneers are named exactly, so they beat the pattern.
#
# What ocerz does instead, for the calls above that have to go through it.
# Process creation, memory mapping and non-local jumps are no longer stubs but
# special records, whose handlers are in src/sysbridge.c.  fork and vfork,
# execve, execv, execvp, execvP, execl, execle and execlp, posix_spawn and
# posix_spawnp, system and system$NOCANCEL, popen and popen$DARWIN_EXTSN, and
# pclose go through ocerz's own fork, exec and spawn, the ones the syscalls take
# in cache mode, so every program a guest starts runs under ocerz in native mode;
# the functions that build posix_spawn's file actions and attributes cross by
# rule, because the objects they fill are the host's and the spawn handler reads
# them with the host's own getters.  mmap, munmap, mprotect and madvise, and
# mach_vm_allocate, mach_vm_deallocate and mach_vm_protect with their vm_
# spellings, go through the code the cache-mode syscalls and Mach traps run, so
# ocerz hears of every change to the address space it tracks; msync, mlock,
# munlock, minherit and mincore cross by rule, because the syscall layer only
# forwards them with the pointer translated, which in native mode's identity map
# is the crossing itself.  setjmp, _setjmp, sigsetjmp, longjmp, _longjmp and
# siglongjmp save and restore the guest's own registers, signal mask and
# floating-point control in Apple's x86_64 jmp_buf layout, and refuse by name a
# jump out of a native function's callback to a setjmp taken outside that call.
# forkpty, daemon and wordexp, which fork or run a shell inside native code, the
# raw ___fork, ___vfork, ___execve, ___posix_spawn, ___mmap, ___munmap and
# ___mprotect entries, the map and remap calls, longjmperror and the ucontext
# calls still name themselves.
#
# The variadic functions whose ellipsis stands for one fixed argument are
# special records too: open, openat and their $NOCANCEL forms,
# open_dprotected_np and openat_dprotected_np take a mode with O_CREAT, fcntl
# and fcntl$NOCANCEL an int or a pointer by command, ioctl a pointer-sized word,
# sem_open a mode and a value and shm_open a mode with O_CREAT, semctl a union
# semun and ulimit a new limit.  Their handlers read that argument where x86-64
# left it, only when the call uses it, and call the host function through its
# real variadic prototype.
#
# csops asks the kernel about the code signature of a process, and no public
# header declares it.  bash asks it about itself as it starts, so a native-mode
# system or popen, which runs the command under /bin/sh and so under bash,
# stopped there.  It crosses with the syscall's own arguments, and the answer
# is about the ocerz process, which is what the syscall layer forwards a
# cache-mode guest's csops to as well.
#
# ---- CoreFoundation ----
# __CFConstantStringClassReference is the isa every CFSTR literal is compiled
# against and no header declares it, so it is bound to the native variable by
# name.  CFBundleGetMainBundle crosses by rule, because ocerz opens
# CoreFoundation with CFProcessPath naming the guest's executable, so the main
# bundle native CoreFoundation answers with is the guest's.  The shapes describe the callback structures the
# collection and run loop constructors copy, word by word, with the version
# the first word must hold; the generator checks every word against the
# structure the header declares, and version 1 against CFRunLoopSourceContext1.
# The struct records name the argument each constructor takes one in.
# CFStringCreateWithFormat and CFStringAppendFormat are format veneers.
#
# ---- libobjc ----
# Message sending.  objc_msgSend and its relatives take whatever arguments the
# method they reach takes, so no signature describes them; they are special
# records whose handlers dispatch the message in ocerz.  The _stret, _fpret and
# _fp2ret forms exist only in x86_64 libobjc, and the arm64 library the host
# runs has none of them, but a guest imports them all the same.  The other
# entry points of the same shape name themselves:
# the _debug and _noarg sends, the objc_msgLookup family, which hands back an
# IMP to jump to with the arguments still in place, method_invoke and
# _objc_msgForward, which continue a send in progress, and
# objc_setForwardHandler, whose handlers are entered the way a send is.  A
# pattern only reaches exports the rules made fn or stub, and the _fixup,
# stret _debug and fpret lookup entries that only x86_64 libobjc has were
# omitted as undeclared and missing on the host, so those are named one by one.
#
# Exceptions.  Throwing an Objective-C exception unwinds through the frames
# between the throw and the catch, and those are translated guest frames and
# ocerz's own, which the native unwinder cannot walk.  So objc_exception_throw
# and objc_exception_rethrow, objc_begin_catch and objc_end_catch,
# objc_terminate, the __objc_personality_v0 personality routine and the legacy
# objc_addExceptionHandler and objc_removeExceptionHandler all name themselves.
# objc_setUncaughtExceptionHandler is special, because it hands back the
# handler it replaces, which the rules refuse as a function pointer returned to
# the guest.
# _OBJC_EHTYPE_id is bound as data like every class's exception type, because a
# guest with an @catch (id) clause references it from its type table whether or
# not anything is ever thrown, and objc_ehtype_vtable for the same reason: the
# compiler points the exception type it emits for a guest's own class at it.
#
# Implementations handed to the guest.  method_getImplementation and
# class_getMethodImplementation answer an IMP, and class_replaceMethod and
# method_setImplementation the one they displaced, and a guest calls what it is
# given: Chromium replaces +[NSObject allocWithZone:] and calls the original on
# every allocation.  A native IMP is arm64 code, so under a plain signature these
# would hand x86 code an address it cannot run.  They are special, and their
# handlers answer the guest's own function when the IMP is one of ocerz's
# trampolines onto it, and a thunk the guest can call for any other.
#
# Class metadata.  Every class a guest defines has its method cache pointing at
# _objc_empty_cache, so a program with a single class of its own fails to bind
# unless that variable is a data export, although no header declares it.
# _objc_empty_vtable, which older toolchains put in the vtable word beside it,
# exists only in x86_64 libobjc and stays omitted.
#
# Runtime hooks.  objc_setHook_* and _objc_setHook_* install functions the
# runtime calls from inside class lookup and method caching, and
# objc_addLoadImageFunc and objc_addLoadImageFunc2 one it calls for every image
# host dyld loads, none of which is a guest image.  _objc_init, _objc_map_images
# and _objc_load_image are dyld's own entry points into the runtime.  The SDK's
# libobjc exports no map_images, load_images or unmap_image under those names.
# objc_setEnumerationMutationHandler takes a function the rules can carry as a
# callback, so it crosses.
#
# Entry points the compiler calls.  ARC, property accessors, object allocation
# and the retain and release fast paths call objc_retain,
# objc_retainAutoreleasedReturnValue, objc_storeStrong, objc_alloc_init,
# objc_opt_class, objc_getProperty and the rest on a program's behalf, and no
# public header declares any of them.  Their fn records carry objc4's own
# signatures, with BOOL written b because it is signed char on x86_64.
# objc_retainBlock is _Block_copy by another name, and is answered with it
# under Blocks below.
#
# ---- Foundation ----
# NSLog is a format veneer and NSLogv stays a stub.  NSFrameAddress,
# NSReturnAddress and NSCountFrames walk the calling thread's native frames,
# which are ocerz's and not the guest's, so they name themselves the way the
# unwinding calls in libSystem do.
#
# ---- CoreGraphics ----
# Four constructors take a structure of callbacks and copy it when they create
# the object: CGFunctionCreate, whose header says so in as many words ("the
# contents of the callbacks structure is copied, so, for example, a pointer to
# a structure on the stack can be passed to this function"), and
# CGDataProviderCreateSequential, CGDataProviderCreateDirect and
# CGPSConverterCreate, whose headers say nothing either way.  All four were
# tested natively: a structure on the stack, rewritten with a second set of
# functions right after the constructor returned, and the object then used -
# drawing a shading, copying a provider's data, running a conversion - called
# the first set every time.  So each has a shape and a struct record.  A
# conversion produces no PDF on current macOS, which no longer reads
# PostScript, but it still calls beginDocument and endDocument.
#
# The version word is not what CoreFoundation's is.  Every one of these
# structures starts with an unsigned int version, four bytes, followed by four
# bytes of padding before the first function pointer, and the bridge reads the
# version as the whole first word.  clang zeroes that padding for a braced
# initializer, and static storage starts zeroed, so either crosses.  A
# structure filled in field by field on the stack is compiled to a four-byte
# store into the version and nothing into the padding, so the high half holds
# whatever the stack held, and the bridge then names the constructor and a
# version it cannot convert and stops with 72.  That is a refusal, never a
# wrong conversion: version 0 only matches when the high half is zero, and then
# the structure really is version 0.
#
# Two callback structures stay stubbed.  CGDataConsumerCallbacks is copied too,
# but it has no version word, so its first word is the putBytes pointer and no
# shape can describe it.  CGPatternCreate copies its CGPatternCallbacks, and the
# shape would be simple, but the structure pointer is its eighth argument,
# behind a CGRect and a CGAffineTransform passed by value, and the bridge only
# converts a structure argument it can find in an integer register with no
# structure by value before it.
#
# ---- AppKit ----
# AppKit's C functions are few, and most of them are drawing calls taking
# rectangles by value, which cross by rule.  One names itself.
#
# NSApplicationMain is an application's whole launch, and it crosses by rule.
# The main bundle it launches from is the guest's, because ocerz gives native
# CoreFoundation the guest's executable as the process path; a guest class is a
# name the native runtime can look up once ocerz has registered it, which it has
# before main; and a native exit runs the guest's atexit handlers, which are on
# the host's list, and flushes the host's stdio, which is the guest's.
# NSApplicationLoad crosses too.
#
# NSAccessibilityRaiseBadArgumentException exists to throw an Objective-C
# exception, which cannot unwind through the guest frames between it and a
# guest's @catch, so it names itself for the same reason objc_exception_throw
# does.  The other NSAccessibility functions take and return objects, strings,
# points and rectangles, and cross.
#
# Nothing else in AppKit's C interface hands native code a function pointer:
# the delegate, selector and context of NSShowAnimationEffect are an object and
# a selector like any target and action, the alert-sheet functions that also
# take them are variadic and already stubs, and nothing takes a structure of
# callbacks.  The private frameworks AppKit re-exports, UIFoundation and
# CollectionViewCore, are folded into AppKit's exports and add classes,
# constants and one declared function, UIFoundation's
# NSConvertGlyphsToPackedGlyphs, which takes nothing but pointers and integers.

library /usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib

special _exit exit
special ___error error
special __exit exit_now
special __Exit exit_now
special _abort abort
special __tlv_bootstrap tlv_bootstrap
special ___stack_chk_fail stack_chk_fail
