# ---- Blocks ----
# A block a guest compiled has x86 code in its invoke word and in its
# descriptor's copy and dispose helpers, and in native mode its isa is the
# native class, because __NSConcreteStackBlock and its siblings bind to the
# host's own variables.  A block parameter or result of a function crosses as
# class k, with the block type's own signature in the braces, and src/blocks.c
# wraps the block for the other side.  The runtime functions a guest imports
# are libclosure's, and the native ones are right for every block but one: a
# guest stack block, whose copy helper _Block_copy would call as arm64 code, and
# a __block variable still on the stack whose keep helper _Block_object_assign
# would.  So _Block_copy, and objc_retainBlock, which is the same function,
# are special records whose handler copies a guest stack block guest-side and
# gives the heap copy a descriptor whose helpers are callback slots, and
# _Block_object_assign is one that does the same for its block and __block
# cases and hands every other case to the native function.  _Block_release and
# _Block_object_dispose cross by rule: the heap blocks and __block variables
# they free have helpers native code can call, whichever side made them.
# _NSConcreteStackBlock and the other classes stay data bound to the native
# variables, so a guest block is a native object that native code can retain.

